Keiran wrote:

> Not to beg the question, but isn't language defined by society? I'm
> not familiar with any Grand Council of Proper Language ruling on which
> new words will be added, archaic terms discarded, and bits of slang
> made proper. Which is more or less where we started, I suppose.

If bits of slang can be "made proper", then there is a concept
of "proper".  There's no Grand Council, but there are people
who study language and they observe the patterns in our language
and those patterns are called "rules of grammar."  

Slang only works in one's little colloquial group.  When one
is speaking or writing for a larger audience, this is when
"proper English" needs to be used, because the "proper" rules
are the _universal_ rules.  If one wants to be clear, then
using universal rules of grammar (thereby avoiding slang
and colloquialisms and such), one has the best chance of
success.


If I know all the rules of grammar, but I feel like saying
"ain't no" while being interview on Jay Leno in a certain 
context in order to emphasize a point, and I'm sure that 
by doing so, not only will no one mis-understand me, but
rather, they will understand me all the more because of
the nuance of the colloquialism, then, by golly, I'm going
to say "There ain't no way a guy can eat a gun powder 
sandwich and live, and that's how I know the Coyote is
and Antichrist!"

Grammatical rules are not laws, so we really can't talk
about "breaking" the rules.  People get confused because
of this.  You don't have to follow the rules, because there
is no Grand Council and no Grammar Police.  So they say
"Great! I can write however I want."  Ok, if that's the
conclusion you want to draw.

But the way to think of it is more like "IF you want to
be understood, then deviate from the universal patterns
carefully".  And how is one going to know when he is
deviating, if he doesn't even know what the rules are?

